URL: https://uninove.emnuvens.com.br/remark/article/view/30973
Purpose: This study aimed to analyze the influence of trust on the intent to use sharing economy services.

Design/methodology/approach: The study is based on a sample of 942 Brazilian consumers aged >18 who are familiar with the sharing economy, including the terminology associated with these services. The theoretical model developed encompasses five constructs based on the sharing business model, and their relationships were evaluated using structural equation modeling.

Originality: It is timely to examine the development of trust within the unique context of the sharing economy business model. This study contributes to the extension of the theoretical domain related to this topic by considering various trust-building mechanisms appropriate for this phenomenon.

Findings: The results demonstrate that trust in the platform, product, and user community positively influences the intention to use shared services, whereas trust in peers does not directly affect the intention to use.

Theoretical and Practical Implications: In this research, trust-building mechanisms are expanded, considering the peculiarities of the sharing economy phenomenon, contributing to a comprehensive understanding of how trust is established in this context. Practically, this study supports companies' strategic decision-making and provides a broader perspective for entrepreneurs intending to establish or expand their businesses within the Brazilian sharing economy sector.
