web_search results for 'Uber cash payment Argentina currency risk transaction failure':

[1] I paid in cash for a trip I requested as a digital payment  | Riders | Uber Help
URL: https://help.uber.com/riders/article/pagu%C3%A9-en-efectivo-un-viaje-que-ped%C3%AD-con-un-m%C3%A9todo-de-pago-digital?nodeId=068b60b3-bc5f-4a70-9ebe-f374473938fa
Before making any payment to the Driver, remember to check the payment method selected in your app. If you choose to pay with a digital payment method, credit card, PayPal, Uber Cash or Mercado Pago (only for Argentina, Chile and Uruguay), it will be paid directly in the app.

So if you choose a digital payment method when you request the trip, but end up paying in cash, you probably paid twice for the same trip.

[2] Martin's blog - Uber in Argentina (2021-03-08T00:00:00.000Z)
URL: https://7c0h.com/blog/new/uber_in_argentina.html
Uber arrived in Argentina working in a grey legal area, as usual. Word of mouth is that Uber refused to be classified as a transport company and insisted on being classified as a digital services company instead. These legal problems led to them being unable to accept Argentinean credit cards for payment. But Uber kept offering the service at a loss, allowing local drivers to accept cash and adding the debt to their driver profile. According to insiders, the drivers were expected to keep Uber's 25% cut aside and transfer it once in a while themselves. Although Uber eventually managed to get access to credit cards, they kept the cash option available.
...
Some drivers decided not to settle their debts with Uber, keeping 100% of the proceeds instead. If and when Uber closes the driver's account they get a new SIM card, send fake documentation, and start with a fresh account that lasts between a week and a month. These drivers accept only cash: they have no bank account data to provide because their data is fake, and they know that it's only a matter of time before their account gets banned anyway.

Because these drivers accept that their account is temporary, none of Uber's typical incentives work. When a passenger pays with credit card the money goes straight to Uber and the driver doesn't see a dime -- it all goes away to settle a debt they had no intention of paying. Therefore, drivers will often contact potential passengers asking how they intend to pay. If the passenger says "credit card", the driver either cancels the trip or straight up ignores the passenger forcing them to cancel. You can take the time and report the driver, but few people do it and all it does is to cause a mild inconvenience to the driver.

[3] How do I pay for Uber in Argentina?
URL: https://www.citizendailypost.com/faq/how-do-i-pay-for-uber-in-argentina
Paying for Uber in Argentina can be complicated due to local banking regulations and foreign currency controls. While Uber officially accepts international credit and debit cards, these transactions are often processed in USD and may be subject to additional taxes or unfavorable conversion rates depending on your bank. Many local users and savvy travelers prefer to pay with cash (which is an option you can select in the app) to avoid the complications of currency conversion or "tourist tax" surcharges. If you choose to use a card, ensure your bank is aware of your travel to avoid fraud blocks. Overall, paying in local currency (Argentine Pesos) using cash is currently the most transparent and commonly used method for travelers to pay for rides without triggering unexpected international transaction fees on their credit statements.

[4] On the Effects of the Availability of Means of Payments: The Case of Uber
URL: https://bfi.uchicago.edu/wp-content/uploads/2020/12/BFI_WP_2020173.pdf
We use three quasi-natural experiments in Mexico and one in Panama to estimate the effects of having the option to pay with cash on Uber rides. The ability to pay in cash affects the demand for rides, which is reflected in large changes in the total number of trips, fares, miles, and number of users after Uber introduced cash payments, particularly in lower-income city blocks. On the other hand, the effects on prices, estimated times of arrival, and competitor pricing are negligible, consistent with the supply of trips being very elastic. Although cash payments naturally increase the fraction of users that pay exclusively with cash, more than half of the users have access to both cards and cash, and alternate between payment methods. We find evidence consistent with cash and card payments being imperfectly substitutable at both the intensive and extensive margins, which magnifies the impact of policies that restrict the availability of payment methods.
...
