The ICD-11 CDDG and the DSM-5 criteria for Autism Spectrum Disorder are similar in their conceptualization of autism as a broad category (“spectrum”) comprising many different

presentations, and in their specific phenomenological requirements of: a) persistent deficits in 
social communication/social interaction; and b) restricted, repetitive and inflexible patterns of 
behaviour, interests or activities. However, although they are intended to identify the same 
people, there are some differences in diagnostic requirements. 
For deficits in social communication, the DSM-5 requires all three of the following: a) 
