Autism spectrum disorder rarely occurs in isolation. It is frequently accompanied by attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD), anxiety disorders, depressive disorders, epilepsy, and difficulties with sleep and eating. Some people have reduced intellectual functioning, while others function within or above the typical range. Sensory hyper- or hyposensitivity (an atypical perception of sounds, light, touch, or textures) can be a major source of strain, even when it is not the first thing others notice.

Diagnosis should take the developmental and cultural context into account. Some behaviors that look atypical in one setting may be adaptive or culturally accepted in another. For this reason a diagnostic assessment relies on a developmental history, observation in more than one situation, and information from people who know the patient across different contexts.
