URL: https://www.ingentaconnect.com/content/routledg/cjhr/2025/00000024/00000002/art00007
Although decision makers frequently implement transitional justice mechanisms after mass killings in hopes of preventing—or at least reducing—future mass atrocities, the literature has not systematically examined whether transitional justice mechanisms are associated with the prevention of or reductions in the incidence of a subsequent mass atrocity. Thus, policymakers lack evidence indicating that transitional justice policies, which aim to prevent human rights abuses, actually reduce the likelihood of future mass atrocities. We address this by using split-population duration models, a mass atrocity dataset, and a transitional justice dataset to test the relationship between transitional justice implementation and mass atrocity onset/recurrence. Our results provide cross-regional, cross-temporal statistical evidence that, in states that have experienced a prior mass killing and then implemented a trial, truth commission, exile, purge, or any combination of these four mechanisms within five years of the end of a conflict episode, the wait-time until the next mass atrocity is four times longer than in countries that experienced a prior mass killing and enacted none of these policies. This finding constitutes an important addition to the fields of transitional justice, peace and security, and mass atrocity prevention. 

