In this article, we seek to reconcile the mixed findings of prior work. We undertake a systematic study of whether adopting postconflict justice influences the likelihood that a postconflict state will return to armed conflict. Put differently, we examine whether variation in the use of postconflict justice can help us understand why some states remain at peace while others return to war. Drawing on the postconflict justice and conflict literatures, we argue that postconflict justice can be broadly classified as either a motivation-or opportunity-addressing process. Motivation postconflict justice includes truth commissions, reparations programs, amnesties, and comprehensive trials (a subset of conflict-related trials is described below). These processes decrease the likelihood of conflict recurrence by, first, mitigating grievances that resulted from the previous conflict and, second, signaling the postconflict government's commitment to peace and reconciliation. Opportunity postconflict justice includes trials that solely target the opposition, exiles, and purges. Through these processes, a postconflict government demonstrates its strength to aggrieved individuals. It therefore increases the opportunity costs for participating in renewed conflict and decreases the likelihood of conflict recurrence.

