Measurement issues, such as the creation of generalizable, transitional justice variables, are a first order priority if transitional justice questions are to be more integrally embedded in larger democratization and peace-building research tracks. This paper examines current efforts to conceptualize and operationalize one set of related, personnel reforms: vetting, purges and lustration measures. Drawing on the transitional justice literature, I develop a rubric to map the areas of conceptual similarity and difference across the measures, and argue that these conceptual distinctions should be reflected in measurement distinctions. I illustrate why measurement choices matter, by comparing the coding decisions in two of the most comprehensive transitional justice datasets to date, namely the Transitional Justice Database (TJD) project and the Post-Conflict Justice (PCJ) Database Project. The TJD compresses all personnel reform measures into a lustration category and the PCJ compresses all such measures into a purge category. I illustrate how these umbrella coding categories affect both case selection and the coding of individual transitional justice mechanisms, with significant empirical implications. In other words, these coding choices affect what we come to think we know about the relationships between transitional justice mechanisms, like purges and lustration, and transition goals, like democracy and respect for human rights. This paper then proposes an alternate, differentiated operationalization of vetting/purges/and lustration, reflecting conceptual distinctions between the measures. I demonstrate systematic variation in the relationships between vetting, purges and lustration measures and democratization and human rights. By conflating the measures, important a…

