### What GPS accuracy is good enough, and how do I get it with a phone?

EUDR doesn’t publish a hard meter threshold. In practice, 5–10 m horizontal accuracy is sufficient to verify deforestation‑free status against 10 m Sentinel or 30 m Landsat layers. With a modern smartphone, you can consistently hit 3–8 m if you:

- Record at the plot’s edge in open sky where possible. Avoid tall shade trees blocking half the sky.
- Use waypoint averaging. Stand still for 20–30 seconds per vertex. Most apps show an accuracy readout; wait until it stabilizes.
- Map during mid‑morning for better satellite geometry. Rainy afternoons under canopy are the worst.
- Bring a power bank. Low battery degrades GPS performance more than people expect.

We log the reported accuracy per point in attributes. Consistency matters more than chasing perfect numbers.
