URL: https://www.fairtrade.net/content/dam/fairtrade/fairtrade-international/standards/small-scale-producer/coffee/Coffee_SPO_Interpretation_note_EN.pdf
Interpretation What is the rule and how does it work? All SPOs certified in Fairtrade coffee are required to provide geolocation data for all members’ plots to Fairtrade International according to the applicable formats and processes in order to meet the Standard requirements. SPOs submit the required geolocation data through Plot Insights. Plot Insights displays the deforestation and protected-area risk status associated with the submitted plots. Where Plot Insights identifies a plot as requiring supporting documentation, the SPO must first confirm that the location and, where applicable, the boundaries of the plot are correct.

If the geolocation information is incorrect, the SPO must correct or remap the plot and submit the 
corrected information through Plot Insights. A new risk analysis will then be generated. 
No Alert Investigation Form is required for an alert resulting from incorrect geolocation information 
unless the new analysis based on the corrected location identifies a case requiring supporting 
documentation. 
Where the geolocation information is correct, the action required depends on the type of risk 
identified. 
An Alert Investigation Form is required where: 
• a deforestation alert is located within the production plot; or 
• the production plot is located within a protected area. The SPO must investigate the case using the applicable Fairtrade Alert Investigation Form and compile the supporting documentation necessary to demonstrate compliance with requirement 3.1.2. An Alert Investigation Form is not required where: 
• evidence of deforestation is identified near but outside the production plot; or 
• the production plot is located near but outside a protected area. Where such a nearby risk is identified and the plot is not already mapped as a p…

