[5] When Autocracies Tremble: A Fuzzy-Set Analysis of Authoritarian Breakdown and Survival under Mass Contention, 1953-2024 by Braulio Braulio :: SSRN (2026-07-14T17:06:30.000Z)
URL: https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=7118179
When does mass contention bring down an authoritarian regime, and why do some regimes survive mobilisation that topples others? This article compares 49 episodes of mass contention against authoritarian regimes in six world regions between 1953 and 2024 using fuzzy-set qualitative comparative analysis (fsQCA), drawing on five conditions established by previous scholarship: coercive defection, elite fragmentation, sustained external support, foreign intervention, and ruling-party institutionalisation. The central finding is an asymmetry. Breakdown follows a single route (the coercive apparatus defects and the ruling elite fragments, each necessary on its own and the two being jointly sufficient where the regime lacks sustained external support) which covers every case of collapse in the dataset. Survival, by contrast, is reached by three routes, each resting on a loyal coercive apparatus and complemented by an institutionalised party, decisive external backing or a cohesive elite. Three common expectations do not hold: the loss of an external patron is not necessary for breakdown, foreign intervention is redundant rather than a second route, and ruling-party institutionalisation matters for survival but not for breakdown. The analysis shows that authoritarian collapse under mass contention is settled in the regime's coercive and elite core and not by grievances alone.

