Do autocratic-mandated leaders erode democratic institutions, and through what channels? To answer these questions, I construct a new database of autocratic executives (prime ministers, presidents) across 62 competitive democracies from 1945 to 2025, using an ex-ante definition based on party campaign manifestos scored by a large language model for the share of content devoted to autocratic rhetoric. First, I show that the election of an autocratic-mandated leader strongly predicts whether a country transitions into autocracy within the following decade, under two independent regime classifications. Second, using local-projections and heterogeneity-robust difference-in-differences, I show that once in power these leaders weaken democratic institutions and judicial constraints on the executive gradually and cumulatively without reversion. They do so through executive non-compliance with the courts and court packing, rather than outright purges of sitting judges, alongside rising government and self-censorship of the media and harassment of journalists. Together this paper's results show that autocratic-mandated leaders are constrained by existing institutional guardrails at first but wear them down over time, and that this trajectory is, to a meaningful degree, visible in their campaign rhetoric before it unfolds. 

