Ratchadamnoen Road and the streets directly feeding into it make up Pai’s tourist core. Staying here puts you within walking distance of the night bazaar, food stalls, and the bus terminal. Budget guesthouses still exist here, tucked behind the cafés, but they fill up fast. If you can book midweek, you’ll find rooms from 250–400 THB per night for a fan room with shared bathroom. The trade-off is noise: the Walking Street runs until midnight and the generators at food stalls start at dusk.
...
Cross the bridge over the Pai River and head a few hundred metres south and east, and accommodation density drops sharply while prices follow. This is where budget bamboo bungalow operations cluster. You wake up to actual birdsong instead of tour operator loudspeakers. A basic bungalow with fan and cold shower here runs 200–350 THB per night on weekdays. It’s a 10-minute walk to town — totally manageable on foot or on a bicycle.
...
### Budget (Under 400 THB per night)

Bamboo bungalows with fan and cold shower, hostel dormitory beds in town, and basic guesthouses in the riverside area. Expect shared bathrooms in the cheapest options. This tier is real and functional — many places at this price are clean, well-maintained, and run by families who’ve been hosting travelers for years. Midweek availability is generally good outside peak season.

### Mid-Range (400–900 THB per night)

Private bungalows with air conditioning and hot showers, smaller resort-style properties in the valley with rice paddy views, and some of the better-located town guesthouses. This is the sweet spot for comfort-to-cost ratio in Pai. At 600–700 THB per night midweek, you can get a genuinely lovely room with a private te…

