[1] Cash, card or ATM in Thailand: what each really costs | ThaiRate
URL: https://thairate.com/en/guide/cash-card-or-atm-in-thailand/
The question comes up before every trip: bring cash to change, or just use the card and the ATM once you land? In Thailand the answer is unusually clear, and it turns on a single number — the 220 baht that practically every Thai ATM charges a foreign card, per withdrawal, on top of whatever your own bank adds.
...
Foreign notes changed at a counter. The only cost is the gap between the counter’s rate and the mid-market rate. At a competitive Bangkok counter that gap is small for US dollars, pounds and Australian dollars alike; the best boards sit within a fraction of a per cent of mid-market, and the difference between the best and worst counter in the same city on the same day is 1–3%. No fixed fee, no minimum. The larger the amount, the less anything else matters.

Paying by card in a shop. Your bank converts the baht amount at the card network’s rate, which is close to mid-market, and then adds its foreign transaction fee if it charges one. That fee is the whole story: anywhere from nothing on a US credit card marketed as no-foreign-fee, to around 3% on many UK and Australian high-street debit cards. Look yours up before you fly. On top of that sits dynamic currency conversion, which we come to below.

Withdrawing from an ATM. Three charges stack here: the Thai machine’s flat 220 baht, your bank’s foreign transaction fee on the amount, and in some cases a separate cash-withdrawal fee from your bank as well. The exchange rate the machine applies is usually fine. It is the fees that make the difference.
...
The machine fee is a flat amount, not a percentage, so the size of each withdrawal decides what it costs you:

