Executive summary On 31 August 2026, Thailand published revisions to its visa exemption and visa on arrival (VoA) policies in the Royal Gazette. The measures will take effect on 15 September 2026. The changes revoke the previous 60-day visa exemption program, reduce the list of countries and territories eligible for visa-exempt entry, introduce a limited 15-day visa exemption program for certain nationalities and significantly restrict access to VoA.
...
Key developments Effective 15 September 2026, the Thai government has implemented the following changes to its visa exemption and entry privileges framework: ▪ Revocation of the 60-day visa exemption previously available to nationals of 93 countries and territories for tourism, short-term work and short-term business visits. ▪ Reduction in the number of countries and territories whose nationals may enter Thailand without a visa for tourism purposes and stay for up to 30 days. The revised program now applies to nationals of 60 countries and territories, and includes Australia, Austria, Bahrain, Belgium, Bulgaria, Bhutan, Brunei Darussalam, Canada, Croatia, Cyprus, Czech Republic, Denmark, Estonia, Fiji, Finland, France, Georgia, Germany, Greece (Hellenic Republic), Hungary, Iceland, Indonesia, India, Ireland, Israel, Italy, Japan, Jordan, Kuwait, Kyrgyzstan, Latvia, Liechtenstein, Lithuania, Luxembourg, Malta, Malaysia, Maldives, Netherlands, New Zealand, Norway, Oman, Philippines, Poland, Portugal, Qatar, Romania, Saudi Arabia, Singapore, Slovakia, Slovenia, South Africa, Spain, Sweden, Switzerland, Taiwan, Türkiye, Ukraine, the United Arab Emirates, the United Kingdom and the United States of America.

