Railay Beach is one of the few places on earth you simply cannot drive to. Cut off from the mainland by sheer limestone karsts, the Railay peninsula is reachable only by boat — and the most iconic way to get there is the Ao Nang long-tail boat. The crossing from Ao Nang Beach or Nopparat Thara Pier takes just 15–20 minutes, gliding past towering cliffs, turquoise water and the occasional monkey-spotted mangrove before the boat noses onto Railay's soft white sand.

Boats are run by the Ao Nang Longtail Boat Association, which keeps prices fixed and posted at beach kiosks, so you pay the same as everyone else — roughly $5–8 USD per person one-way on a shared boat, or about $25–30 for a private charter for the whole boat. Just walk up to a kiosk on Ao Nang Beach, tell them Railay (or Phra Nang / Tonsai), and you'll be matched onto the next departure. Departures run every 15–30 minutes from around 08:00 until about 18:00, all year round, with the calmest seas from November to April.
