Wat Arun Ratchawararam, the Temple of Dawn, rises on the west bank of the Chao Phraya River, in Bangkok Yai district, opposite Wat Pho. An ancient temple dating back to the Ayutthaya period, it was the royal temple of King Rama II and housed the Emerald Buddha before it was moved to Wat Phra Kaew. Its great central prang, clad in fragments of porcelain and ceramics, is one of the most photographed silhouettes in the city. The temple is open every day from 8am to 6pm; admission is 200 baht for foreign visitors (prices checked in October 2026). Allow about 1 hour to walk around the prang, climb up to its terrace and see the ordination hall. Most visitors arrive by ferry from Tha Tien pier, a crossing of just a few minutes. See also: Wat Kalayanamit, restaurants with a view and Santa Cruz Church and Kudi Chin.
...
