On 19 January 2023 the State Railway of Thailand (SRT) moved 52 long-distance rapid and express trains from Hua Lamphong to the new Krung Thep Aphiwat Central Terminal in the north of the city, according to the Tourism Authority of Thailand. All the northern and northeastern expresses that stop at Ayutthaya now start there. Krung Thep Aphiwat sits beside the MRT Blue Line and the SRT Red Line, close to Chatuchak, so it is easy to reach from most hotels.

Hua Lamphong did not close. The same TAT notice says ordinary trains, commuter trains and special tourist trains continued to use it, calling at Sam Sen and Krung Thep Aphiwat on the way north. These are the slowest and cheapest trains to Ayutthaya. If you are staying near Chinatown or Silom, Hua Lamphong (MRT Hua Lamphong station on the Blue Line) can still be the closer start.

The practical rule: an express, rapid or BNEX train means Krung Thep Aphiwat; a third-class ordinary train may start at Hua Lamphong and pick up at Krung Thep Aphiwat too. Ask at the ticket counter which platform your train uses, because the big terminal has separate platforms for northern and southern lines. Our guide to the BTS and MRT explains how to get to either station by metro.
