The heart of the Zoom PodTrak P8 is its six microphone preamps, and they are the strongest part of the package. Each XLR input offers 0 to 70 dB of gain with an equivalent input noise figure of −124 dBu (IHF-A) measured at +70 dB into a 150-ohm source. In practical terms that means the P8 can drive notoriously low-output dynamic broadcast microphones — the Shure SM7B and Heil PR 40 are the usual test cases — up to a healthy speaking level without an external inline preamp such as a Cloudlifter or FetHead. That is a meaningful saving in India, where a booster adds several thousand rupees per channel to a six-mic setup. Frequency response measures 20 Hz to 20 kHz within −1.0 dB at 44.1 kHz, which comfortably covers the full vocal range with room to spare.

Where the P8 shows its age is resolution. Recording is fixed at 16-bit/44.1 kHz WAV, mono or stereo. For spoken-word podcasting that is entirely adequate — broadcast radio has run at similar or lower resolution for decades, and the final MP3 or AAC your listeners hear will be far more lossy than the source. But if you plan aggressive noise reduction, heavy compression, or you want to salvage a badly set gain stage in post, the extra headroom of a 24-bit file genuinely helps. Set your levels properly on the way in — the P8’s per-channel limiter and level meters make this straightforward — and the difference becomes academic. Onboard low-cut, tone adjustment, compressor/de-esser and a noise-reduction mode that ducks unused mics all help you get a broadcast-ready mix before the file ever reaches a computer.
