As of 2024, an estimated 800 million people globally do not have official identification—primarily people in lower-income countries and marginalized and vulnerable groups: 

- Most people without an ID live in low-income (LIC) and lower-middle-income (LMIC) economies in Sub-Saharan Africa and South Asia. Sub-Saharan Africa accounts for more than half of the global unidentified population.
- Over half the population without official identification are children whose births have not been registered. 
- Women living in LICs are 5.1 percentage points less likely to have an ID than men, an improvement from the 8-percentage point gender gap in LICs in 2021. 
- The gap in ID ownership is also significant for other vulnerable groups: adults in LICs are less likely to have an ID when they have only primary schooling or less, are out of the workforce, or live in rural areas.
