Passwords are not insecure because people are careless with them – they are structurally broken. The underlying model relies on a shared secret between client and server, which can be intercepted, guessed, or compromised through data leaks. Passkeys address precisely this vulnerability and solve the problem at an architectural level: instead of a password, a cryptographic key pair is used. The private key never leaves the device; only the public key resides on the server.

