In the end of August 2017 to the light came the security risk in the ID card’s processor chip 
hardware firmware encryption key generation function, which meant that in case of existance of 
enough computing power it is possible from the authentication and digital signature public key 
to find out user’s private key. As a result it is possible to electronically authenticate and give 
digital signatures without having ID card’s chip and without knowing PIN codes, i.e., use ID 
card in the electronic environment without its owner’s knowledge. 
Security risk concerns all from 2014-10-16 issued identification cards, as well as residence 
permit cards and digital identity cards and e-resident’s digital identity cards (hereinafter ID 
card). Mobile-ID and ID cards issued before 2014-10-16 are not affected by the security flaw. 
This analysis discusses ID card security risk related legal questions stemming from the validity 
rights. The analysis does not disclose details resulting from the contracts between the parties, 
since the goal of the analysis is to clarify validity rights bottlenecks assessing need for changes 
of the regulation in the field. 
