- The RP could put identifying information into the user handle; however, the RP must already know this information since the user handle is chosen by the RP. WebAuthn allows the RP to re-discover this identifier in a new session, but does not introduce a way for the RP to initially discover this information. WebAuthn strongly recommends against putting identifying information into the user handle since the user handle may be discoverable outside of the Web platform; for example after a credential is stored on an external USB security key, the user handle may be discoverable by the USB host. On the Web, user handles are only revealed to the first party (the RP that created them), never to third parties (other web domains).
- Each credential ID is a (probabilistically) unique identifier for a credential, and thus a unique identifier for the user who created that credential. Like user handles, credential IDs are only revealed to the first party on the web, but may be discoverable by other entities outside the Web platform by direct interaction with the authenticator.
