## How the openings are organized

 Every opening here carries one of the ECO codes, a system that files chess openings into five volumes: A — flank openings such as the English and the Réti, plus the Dutch and the Benoni; B — semi-open games such as the Sicilian, the Caro-Kann and the Pirc; C — open games after 1.e4 e5, plus the French Defense; D — closed and semi-closed games after 1.d4 d5, such as the Queen's Gambit, plus the Grünfeld; E — Indian defenses such as the Catalan, the Nimzo-Indian and the King's Indian. 

 Names and move orders come from the Lichess chess-openings dataset: 3,690 named lines spread over almost all of the 500 ECO codes. Each opening page shows the moves on an interactive board, where the line sits in the opening tree, its sub-variations, and how it scores in Lichess games between players rated 1600 to 2500 — so you can see how an opening actually does, not just how it starts. 

 Know the moves but not the name? Search by moves or by FEN. Want to practice? Every line can be drilled from memory, or played…

