The Maroczy Bind carries the name of a player who never set it up with the white pieces. As Malakhov points out, Géza Maroczy — oddly enough — never played the structure as White at all; he appears on the Black side of the game that gave it its name, Swiderski–Maroczy, Monte Carlo 1904.

It is a fitting emblem for a structure that always had to be read from both sides of the board. The same two pawns on c4 and e4 can mean a slow, suffocating squeeze or a springboard for counterplay, depending entirely on who understands the position better.

That double vision is the premise of The Maroczy Bind - Strategic Foundations by GM Vladimir Malakhov. It is a structures course, not a repertoire — there are no long forcing lines to memorise. Instead it studies the formation itself: what each side is trying to do, which pieces to exchange and which to keep, where the pawn breaks live, and the exact moment a natural-looking move becomes a serious error.
...
The Bind is reached most directly through the Accelerated Dragon (1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4 g6 5.c4), but the same pawn structure appears from other Sicilian lines, from the English Opening, and even from 1.d4 systems. Learning it once pays off across a wide stretch of your repertoire, and recognising it whatever the move order is part of the work.

Space is the whole story. From the main tabiya (1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4 g6 5.c4 Bg7 6.Be3 Nf6 7.Nc3 O-O 8.Be2 d6 9.O-O), White takes d5 under control and plays for queenside expansion with b4 and, when allowed, the central break f4-e5. Black regroups with ...Bd7-Bc6-Nd7 and ...a5, fights for the c5-square, and waits to strike with ...b5 or ...e6-d5.

