This case study investigates the making and the experience of the Icelandic law on paid parental leave that was adopted in early 2000 and has been successful in terms of its impact on gender equality. For decades, Icelandic society has been moving towards increased gender equality. Political parties have overall agreed on that goal and the Icelandic parliament has initiated laws and campaigns to promote gender equality spurred on by diverse women’s movements and the trade unions. This has been fairly successful, as can be seen, for example, in the relatively high political representation of women, which contributes to putting Iceland on top of the Global Gender Gap Index, compiled by the World Economic Forum, a position Iceland has held for 12 consecutive years. Still, in the late twentieth century, policies to increase gender equality mainly focused on enhancing the position of women in politics, education and the labour market while not much happened at home, where women shouldered the main responsibility for the care of children. Even though mothers could transfer part of their paid parental leave entitlements to the father, this hardly ever happened (Eydal and Gíslason 2008).
