Female Labour Force Participation (FLFP) in India has long been characterised by persistently low rates despite sustained economic growth. Drawing on successive rounds of the Periodic Labour Force Survey (PLFS,2017–18 to 2024–25), this study examines trends and structural patterns in FLFP. FLFP among women aged 15–59 years is measured using the Usual Status (principal and subsidiary status, ps+ss) approach. Trend analysis, comparative analysis, and percentage-point decomposition are employed to assess rural–urban differentials, interstate disparities, employment status composition, and sectoral distribution of female employment. The findings reveal a substantial recovery in FLFP over the study period, concentrated predominantly among rural women. However, it is accompanied by a marked shift towards self-employment and agriculture, limited expansion of regular wage employment, and a persistent absence of formal sector opportunities. Interstate disparities indicate that women's labour market outcomes are shaped by structural and socio-cultural factors beyond aggregate economic growth. The study concludes that rising participation rates alone are insufficient indicators of female economic empowerment, and calls for gender-responsive policies that prioritise access to decent, productive, and formally protected employment while addressing regional inequalities in female work.
