The near-doubling of women’s LFPR represents one of Vision 2030’s most dramatic social transformations. Key drivers include the lifting of the driving ban (2018), guardianship reforms (2019), Hadaf wage subsidies for female employees, the opening of entertainment and hospitality sectors that provide female employment, and shifting cultural attitudes. Female unemployment declined from 31.7 percent (2018) to 12.1 percent (Q1 2025).

Reaching the revised 40 percent target requires continued progress at approximately 1 percentage point per year — achievable but not guaranteed, particularly as the “easiest” gains (women entering the workforce for the first time) give way to more structural challenges including childcare, workplace culture, and sectoral concentration.
