[7] Vision 2030 in Action: How the 2018 Driving Reform Shaped Women’s Labor Participation in Urban and Rural Saudi Arabia (2025-12-05T00:00:00.000Z)
URL: https://exa.ai/library/publication/mggt16z4lxh
June 2018 marked a watershed moment in Saudi Arabia's contemporary history when the country finally granted women the right to drive after a prohibition lasting for many years. The lifting of the ban, which was a key part of the Vision 2030 program, was intended not only to expand women's mobility and freedom but also to eliminate, to a great extent, one of the main obstacles to their economic participation— limited access to independent means of transport. Before the ban was lifted, the labour force participation rate (LFP) for women in Saudi Arabia was very low, at about 20% in 2018 (World Bank, 2019). In 2023, however, the official statistics from the General Authority for Statistics (GASTAT) reported a considerable increase in female LFP, reaching almost 36%. This development is a strong indication of the country's rapid movement towards the Vision 2030 target of greater gender diversity in the labour market. Although the country's overall path shows significant improvement, this action does not have the same geographic impact. There are still vast regional differences, with metropolitan areas like Riyadh and Makkah showing a steady, more substantial rise in female employment compared with rural areas and peripheral areas such as Jazan and Najran. The latter situation poses a significant empirical question: how much did the 2018 driving reform affect women differently across regions and in people's different economic situations? This research paper addresses the issue using GASTAT Labour Force Survey data from 2017 to 2023 and focuses on the regional distribution of female employment increases after the reform. The study applies both descriptive trend analysis and regression modelling to separate the reform's impacts in urban and rural areas. The findings indicate…

