In H1 2024, the strong post-COVID-19 labor market recovery returned to its long-term trend. Employment growth, which was around 370,000 in H1 2023, slowed to 280,000 in H2 2023 and fell to 220,000 in H1 2024. Job growth fell below 100,000 in May, and in June it increased only slightly on a month-over-month basis. In addition, the number of unemployed people increased and the decline in the economically inactive population slowed. The positive employment signals from quantitative indicators in 2023 have generally weakened in 2024. The labor market in H1 2024 was characterized by different trends by gender. The employment rate for men decreased, while the employment rate for women increased. Since 2023, the participation rate of women in their 30s has continued to increase, as has the number of women employed in the services sector, which has a high proportion of women. However, the rate of increase is declining, with the increase in the female employment rate falling from 1.4%p in H1 2023 to 0.7%p in 2024.
